From 2c09418cab0eda6d1ae47eaeab08a81cf6d9b959 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Majid Arasskhani <M.Arasskhani@gosilkroad.net>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 15:00:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/22] fix(auth): SAML SP refuses unsigned, unsolicited and
 replayed responses

Bug: the admin form could switch off strict validation and the signed-
assertion requirement (an unsigned assertion is a forged login), the ACS
never told python3-saml which AuthnRequest it was expecting (InResponseTo
was not checked, so a captured Response could be replayed and IdP-initiated
responses were accepted), and nothing remembered assertion ids.

Change:
- strict and wantAssertionsSigned are forced on; ALLOW_INSECURE_SAML=false
  (new, env) is the only way to honour the admin toggles. public_view reports
  security_locked and the UI disables the two checkboxes.
- wantMessagesSigned stays off on purpose: most IdPs sign the Assertion, not
  the Response envelope; strict mode already requires one signature and
  checks InResponseTo/Destination/Audience inside the signed assertion.
- /saml/login remembers the AuthnRequest id in Redis (saml:req:<id>, TTL
  SAML_REQUEST_TTL_SECONDS=600). /saml/acs reads InResponseTo, consumes the
  request exactly once (missing/unknown -> 401) and passes the id to
  process_response so the library verifies it against the signed assertion.
- Accepted assertion ids are kept (SET NX) until their NotOnOrAfter; a
  second use -> 401. Every refusal writes a saml_response_rejected audit row.
- The store fails closed: Redis down -> 503, no per-worker memory fallback
  (it would randomly reject logins or let replays through across workers).

Tests: tests/test_saml_hardening.py builds real Responses signed with a
generated IdP key: signed+bound accepted; unsigned rejected even when the
admin unticked the box; InResponseTo mismatch rejected by the library;
unsolicited refused; endpoint refuses the same Response twice and the same
assertion under a fresh request; 503 without Redis.

